"OP-00086: Johnson's derivative-realizability conjecture — clopen.solutions (https://clopen.solutions/OP-00086/)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; The problem below is Johnson's conjecture that the critical points of a realizable list, the zeros of the derivative of its characteristic polynomial counted with multiplicity, are again realizable. ... Disproof · verified · canonical statement matched · posted on GitHub 13 Sep 2026\n\nOP-00086 Lean verified: disproved\n\n# Johnson's derivative-realizability conjecture\n\nSubmit a result\n\nFor a real square matrix $A$ let $p_A(z) = \\det(zI - A)$. A list of $n$ complex numbers is realizable if it is the spectrum of an entrywise nonnegative $n \\times n$ matrix. The problem below is Johnson's conjecture that the critical points of a realizable list, the zeros of the derivative of its characteristic polynomial counted with multiplicity, are again realizable. Hoover, McCormick, Paparella and Thrall record that Cronin and Laffey settled it for $n \\le 4$, and for $n \\le 6$ when $\\operatorname{tr} A = 0$.\n\nProblem. For every integer $n \\ge 5$ and every entrywise nonnegative $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$, is there an entrywise nonnegative $B \\in \\mathbb{R}^{(n-1) \\times (n-1)}$ with $$\\det(zI - B) = \\frac{1}{n}\\, p_A'(z)$$ as polynomials in $z$? Neither $A$ nor $B$ need be symmetric or diagonalizable, and $B$ must have order exactly $n - 1$: appending zero eigenvalues is not allowed.\n\nSource: Johnson (personal communication, 2015), recorded as Conjecture 1.2 by Hoover, McCormick, Paparella and Thrall (2018); Monov (2008) had asked the weaker question that allows extra zero eigenvalues. Stated for n ≥ 5 by OpenProblemsInNLA.\n\nImported from github.com/ajt60gaibb/OpenProblemsInNLA.\n\n## Classification\n\nPrimary\n    NA — Numerical Analysis\nSecondary\n    SP\nKeywords\n    critical points, inverse eigenvalue problems, nonnegative matrices, OpenProblemsInNLA\n\n## References\n    \n    @article{hoover2018,\n      author        = {Hoover, Sarah L. and McCormick, Daniel A. and Paparella, Pietro and Thrall, Amber R.},\n      title         = {On the realizability of the critical points of a realizable list},\n      journal       = {Linear Algebra Appl.},\n      volume        = {555},\n      year          = {2018},\n      pages         = {301--313},\n      doi           = {10.1016/j.laa.2018.06.024},\n      eprint        = {1712.05454},\n      archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n    }\n    @article{monov2008,\n      author  = {Monov, Vladimir V.},\n      title   = {A family of symmetric polynomials of the eigenvalues of a matrix},\n      journal = {Linear Algebra Appl.},\n      volume  = {429},\n      number  = {8-9},\n      year    = {2008},\n      pages   = {2199--2208},\n      doi     = {10.1016/j.laa.2008.06.014},\n    }\n    @article{cronin2014,\n      author  = {Cronin, Anthony G. and Laffey, Thomas J.},\n      title   = {Perturbations on constructible lists preserving realizability in the {NIEP} and questions of {M}onov},\n      journal = {Linear Algebra Appl.},\n      volume  = {445},\n      year    = {2014},\n      pages   = {206--222},\n      doi     = {10.1016/j.laa.2013.12.003},\n    }\n    @misc{openproblemsinnla-is03,\n      author       = {Townsend, Alex},\n      title        = {{IS-03}: Johnson's derivative-realizability conjecture},\n      howpublished = {Open Problems in Numerical Linear Algebra, GitHub repository},\n      year         = {2026},\n      url          = {https://github.com/ajt60gaibb/OpenProblemsInNLA/blob/main/eigenvalues-and-inverse-problems/IS-03/README.md},\n      note         = {Read at commit e375a6f, 2026-09-30},\n    }\n\n## Resolution claims\n\nIS-03: a seven-dimensional derivative-realizability counterexample v1 GitHub ↗\n\nGeorge Stepaniants\n\nComplete negative answer to Johnson's original universal normalized-characteristic-polynomial derivative realizability conjecture, with genuine arbitrary-matrix trace certificates and the unchanged order-seven source witness.\n\nDisproof · verified · canonical statement matched · posted on GitHub 13 Sep 2026\n\nA seven-dimensional counterexample to derivative realizability v1 GitHub ↗\n\nMatthew J. Colbrook\n\nLet $C_k$ be the permutation matrix of a directed $k$-cycle, and let $A=\\operatorname{diag}(\\tfrac12,C_2,C_4)$. This is an entrywise nonnegative real $7\\times7$ matrix with characteristic polynomial $p(z)=(z-\\tfrac12)(z^2-1)(z^4-1)$. The normalized derivative $q=p'/7$ is not the characteristic polynomial of any entrywise nonnegative real $6\\times6$ matrix. Newton's identities give the power sums $s_k$ of the roots of $q$ as exact rationals, and $s_7=-8593/823543<0$. If a …\n\nDisproof · not verified · fidelity not established · posted on GitHub 11 Sep 2026\n\n## Status history\n\nWhen  | Change  | By  | Reason\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n2026-10-05  | Needs verification → Lean verified  | —  | Verified here: OpenProblemsInNLA @ e375a6f, IS-03/lean/.\n\n## Citing this problem\n\nThis identifier is permanent. It will not change if the problem is solved, reclassified or withdrawn.\n    \n    OP-00086, \"Johnson's derivative-realizability conjecture\", clopen.solutions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWestern Canada Linear Algebra Meeting (https://pims.math.ca/files/final_report/WCLAMProgram.pdf)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; 6 ... On the realizability of the critical points of a realizable list ... the kth-moments of the list of critical points of a realizable list are nonnegative. Johnson ... conjecture, and consequently Monovs conjecture, is established for a variety of important ... three matrix analog of Gerstenhaber’s theorem for other inﬁnite families of examples.\nOn the realizability of the critical points of a realizable list\nThe nonnegative inverse eigenvalue problem (NIEP) is to characterize the spectra of en-\ntrywise nonnegative matrices. A ﬁnite multiset (herein list) of complex numbers is called\nrealizable if it is the spectrum of an entrywise nonnegative matrix. Monov conjectured that\nthe kth-moments of the list of critical points of a realizable list are nonnegative. Johnson\nfurther conjectured that the list of critical points must be realizable. In this talk, Johnsons\nconjecture, and consequently Monovs conjecture, is established for a variety of important\ncases including Ciarlet spectra, Suleimanova spectra, spectra realizable via companion ma-\ntrices, and spectra realizable via similarity by a complex Hadamard matrix. Additionally,\nwe prove a result on differentiators and trace vectors, and use it to provide an alternate proof\nof a result due to Malamud and a generalization of a result due to Kushel and Tyaglov on\ncirculant matrices. Implications for further research are discussed.\nJenna Rajchgot, University of Saskatchewan\nOn the algebra generated by three commuting matrices\nIn the early 1960s, Gerstenhaber proved that the algebra generated by two commuting\nd × d matrices has vector space dimension at most d. The analog of this statement for four\nor more commuting matrices is false. The three matrix version remains open.\nAfter providing some history and context, I’ll translate this three commuting matrix\nstatement into an equivalent statement about certain modules over polynomial rings, and\nprove that this commutative-algebraic reformulation is true in special cases. I’ll end with\nsome combinatorial questions about plane partitions, which, if answered, would settle the\nthree matrix analog of Gerstenhaber’s theorem for other inﬁnite families of examples.\nThis is joint work with Matthew Satriano.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe simple ordering conjecture for zero-sum subsets of abelian groups | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/337090/the-simple-ordering-conjecture-for-zero-sum-subsets-of-abeli)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # The simple ordering conjecture for zero-sum subsets of abelian groups ... ## The simple ordering conjecture for zero-sum subsets of abelian groups\n\n# The simple ordering conjecture for zero-sum subsets of abelian groups\n\nAbout 9 years old · traced to 2017\n\nLet $(G,+)$(G,+) be an abelian group. Let $A$A be a finite subset of $G\\setminus\\{0\\}$G∖{0} such that no $2$2-subset $\\{x,-x\\}${x,−x} is contained in $A$A, and suppose that\n\n$$\\sum_{a\\in A}a=0.$$a∈A∑​a=0.\n\nA simple ordering of $A$A is an ordering of its elements whose successive partial sums are distinct. Simple ordering conjecture. There exists a simple ordering of the elements of $A$A. This conjecture would imply that every Heffter array is simple; its validity was proposed in earlier work, while the existence of square integer Heffter arrays is known. The general assertion remains open.\n\nEquivalent formulations 1 Other wordings\n\nOther statements of this same problem, merged from separate entries. Each is equivalent to the statement above — proving any one settles them all.\n\n  1. ## The simple ordering conjecture for zero-sum subsets of abelian groups\n\nLet $(G,+)$(G,+) be an abelian group. Let $A$A be a finite subset of $G\\setminus\\{0\\}$G∖{0} such that no $2$2-subset $\\{x,-x\\}${x,−x} is contained in $A$A and\n\n$$\\sum_{a\\in A}a=0.$$a∈A∑​a=0.\n\nA simple ordering of $A$A is an ordering of its elements whose successive partial sums are distinct. Simple ordering conjecture. There exists a simple ordering of the elements of $A$A. This conjecture concerns sequencing zero-sum subsets of abelian groups under the condition that no element and its inverse both occur; its status is not established by the supplied text.\n\nsource: Simone Costa, Fiorenza Morini, Anita Pasotti and Marco Antonio Pellegrini, “A generalization of Heffter arrays”, arXiv:1906.03932 (2019).\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nSimone Costa, Fiorenza Morini, Anita Pasotti and Marco Antonio Pel